{"task_id": "Mbpp/84", "solution": "def sequence(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth number in the Newman-Conway sequence.\n    The sequence is defined by P(1) = 1, P(2) = 1, and P(n) = P(P(n - 1)) + P(n - P(n - 1)) for n > 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        # According to the problem constraints, we should assume valid positive integer input.\n        # This case should ideally be handled by validation, but per instructions, no exception is raised.\n        # However, the sequence is defined for n >= 1.\n        return 0 # Or some other default behavior, but adhering strictly to constraints.\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize a list to store the sequence values, using 1-based indexing for convenience.\n    # p[0] will be unused, p[1] stores P(1), p[2] stores P(2), etc.\n    p = [0] * (n + 1)\n    p[1] = 1\n    p[2] = 1\n\n    # Calculate values from P(3) up to P(n)\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        p[i] = p[p[i - 1]] + p[i - p[i - 1]]\n\n    return p[n]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/84", "solution": "def sequence(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth number in the Newman-Conway sequence using dynamic programming.\n    P(1) = 1, P(2) = 1, P(n) = P(P(n-1)) + P(n-P(n-1)) for n > 2.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 2:\n        # Base cases: P(1) = 1, P(2) = 1\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize a list to store sequence values, using 1-based indexing.\n    # p[0] is unused, p[i] stores the i-th term.\n    p = [0] * (n + 1)\n    p[1] = 1\n    p[2] = 1\n\n    # Calculate terms from P(3) up to P(n)\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        p[i] = p[p[i-1]] + p[i - p[i-1]]\n\n    return p[n]"}
