{"task_id": "Mbpp/448", "solution": "def cal_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of Perrin numbers from P(0) up to P(n).\n    This interprets 'sum of perrin numbers' for input n to include P(0) through P(n).\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 3  # Sum of P(0)\n    if n == 1:\n        return 3  # Sum of P(0) + P(1) = 3 + 0\n    if n == 2:\n        return 5  # Sum of P(0) + P(1) + P(2) = 3 + 0 + 2\n\n    # Initialize with the first three Perrin numbers\n    perrin_numbers = [3, 0, 2]\n\n    # Generate subsequent Perrin numbers up to P(n)\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        next_perrin = perrin_numbers[i - 2] + perrin_numbers[i - 3]\n        perrin_numbers.append(next_perrin)\n\n    return sum(perrin_numbers)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/448", "solution": "def cal_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of Perrin numbers from P(0) up to P(n) (inclusive).\n    This function adheres to the specific requirement cal_sum(9) == 49.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        # Perrin numbers are typically defined for non-negative integers.\n        # Returning 0 for negative input as per constraint to not raise exceptions.\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize a list to store Perrin numbers from P(0) to P(n).\n    # The size will be n + 1.\n    perrin = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    # Set the initial values for the Perrin sequence: P(0)=3, P(1)=0, P(2)=2.\n    if n >= 0:\n        perrin[0] = 3\n    if n >= 1:\n        perrin[1] = 0\n    if n >= 2:\n        perrin[2] = 2\n\n    # Calculate subsequent Perrin numbers using the recurrence relation: P(i) = P(i-2) + P(i-3).\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        perrin[i] = perrin[i-2] + perrin[i-3]\n\n    # Sum all calculated Perrin numbers from P(0) to P(n).\n    return sum(perrin)"}
